@meta
id: 15-13
title: Collect the open items
chapter: 15
type: lesson
skills: global-copy
par: 23
minutes: 5
vscode: search for [ ], copy each line, scroll to the bottom, paste, scroll back up
open: sprint-notes.md

@brief
Friday. The sprint notes have a section per day and a `## Carry over`
heading at the bottom that is still empty. Every unticked item from the
week, the `- [ ]` lines, needs to appear under that heading, in the
order it was written. The daily sections keep their copies; carry over
is a summary, not a move.

`:m` moved lines. Its sibling `:t` copies them, and `:t$` copies to the
end of the file. Under `:g`, that collects a copy of every match at the
bottom, in order, for the same reason `:m$` did last lesson.

@keys
| `:g/pattern/t$` | copy every matching line to the end of the file, in order |
| `:t$` | copy the current line to the end |
| `\[` | a literal square bracket in a pattern |

@hints
Describe an open item exactly. It starts with a dash, a space, an open
bracket, a space and a close bracket. Ticked items have an `x` where the
space is.
--
Square brackets mean something in a pattern, so the opening one needs a
backslash. The closing one does not, but escaping it too does no harm.
--
`:g/^- \[ \]/t$` and Enter.

@solution
    :g/^- \[ \]/t$<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Nineteen keystrokes, and the six open items are copied to the bottom in
the order they appear.

`t` for copy is old vi: it stands for "to", as in "copy this line to
there". `:t$` is the whole idea of `:g//t$`; it is a report generator.
Any time you want a list of matching lines gathered somewhere without
disturbing the original, this is the shape. Collect every `TODO` at the
bottom of a file before a review, every `throw exception(` in a
provider to compare with the error codes, every failing test name from
a log.

On the bracket: `[` opens a character class in a regex, so an unescaped
`[ ]` would mean "a single space" rather than the three characters you
want. The `\[` makes it literal. Ticked items have `[x]`, so the space
inside is what separates open from done.

@start sprint-notes.md
